.he "imd (5)                       10/6/26                       imd (5)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
imd
- ImageDisk floppy image file format
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
An
.bd
imd
file holds a whole disk, cylinder by cylinder and head by head, the way
Dave Dunfield's ImageDisk utility recorded it.  The simulator and the host
tools read these to get at a floppy, so the format is the one piece every
driver shares.  The authority for it is the header comment in
.bd
include/imd.h,
and the reader is
.bd
lib/imdlib.c.
.sp
A file is a text comment followed by a run of track records.  The comment
runs up to a control-z (0x1a), and every image in this tree opens it with
the program name, so the loader first checks that the file begins
.bd
"IMD ".
Anything that does not is taken for a flat sector image, and a file that
cannot be opened at all for a blank disk; both are described under
.bd
FALLBACKS
below.
.sp
Each track record is a five byte header, then its maps, then the data for
its sectors:
.sp
.nf
.bd 6
mode          1 byte
cylinder      1 byte
head          1 byte
numsecs       1 byte
secsize       1 byte
.sp
secmap        numsecs bytes
cylmap        numsecs bytes, only if head has bit 0x80 set
headmap       numsecs bytes, only if head has bit 0x40 set
.sp
then for each of the numsecs sectors, a one byte data code
and the sector's data, as that code directs.
.fi
.sp
.bd
mode
is the FM/MFM density: 0 for single density, 1 for double.  The reader
hands the byte straight back as the track's density.
.sp
.bd
cylinder
and
.bd
head
address the track.  The low six bits of the head byte are the head number;
the two high bits say whether the optional cylinder and head maps follow
the sector map.  These maps carry alternate cylinder and head numbers for
each sector and are never seen in this tree, but the reader honours them.
.sp
The cylinder field is one byte, so a saved image cannot address a cylinder
above 255 - a floppy era choice, as Dunfield's format never needed more.
That byte is what is written, not the working limit: in core the track
array is indexed by an
.bd
int
cylinder and grows to whatever is written, so a disk of more than 255
cylinders is read and written correctly in memory.  Persistence is what is
capped - the saved file and the delta key each keep the cylinder in one
byte, so an image past 255 would have to be read back by counting tracks
rather than trusting the field.
.sp
.bd
numsecs
is how many sectors the track holds.
.sp
.bd
secsize
is a shift, not a byte count.  The sector size is
.bd
128 << secsize,
so 0 is 128 bytes, 1 is 256, 2 is 512, and 3 is 1024.  A track may differ
from its neighbours, which is how CP/M puts a single density first track
on an otherwise double density disk.
.sp
.bd
secmap
is the sector numbers in the order they were recorded.  Numbering need not
start at one and need not be dense; the driver asks for
.bd
imd_firstsec
when it walks a track rather than assuming.  When a format is applied, the
map is rebuilt as
.bd
firstsec + i.
.sp
The data code for a sector is one of:
.sp
.nf
.bd 3
0       absent - no data follows; the sector reads as zeros
1       data - secsize bytes of sector data follow
2       fill - one byte follows; the whole sector is that byte
.fi
.sp
Absent sectors are how a track records fewer sectors than
.bd
numsecs
or a hole in the map, and they are materialised as zeros on read.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
WRITING AND THE DELTA
.in +5
.br
An imd file is never rewritten in place.  Writes go to a sibling key store
named after the image with
.bd
-delta
appended, so loading an image is first reading the baseline and then
overlaying the delta.  The store carries no geometry of its own; each key
is three bytes - cylinder, head, and a sector slot - or the same pair with
.bd
0xff
in the slot for the track's format record.  A format record's value is
four bytes: the sector count, the sector size shift, the mode, and the
first sector number.  A sector's value is the sector data.  Format records
are applied before sector data on load, since the data is keyed by slot
and sized by the format the record just set.
.sp
A writable image takes an advisory lock on the image for the life of the
process, so a short lived tool and a long lived simulator contend on the
same disk the same way.  A format grows the image cylinder by cylinder as
the disk learns its shape.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FALLBACKS
.in +5
.br
A file that does not begin
.bd
"IMD "
is read as a raw sector image: the sectors in order, cylinder by cylinder
and head by head within each cylinder, and the same track structures built
that an imd file would have produced, delta included.  The geometry has to
come from somewhere a flat file does not carry, so it is recognised first
by the device number in the file's name and then by its size, per the
table in
.bd
lib/imdlib.c.
A missing file is a blank disk, empty until it is formatted.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
